__registerContent("newspaper/polity/2026-07-24/right-to-protest", {"notes":{"news-2026-07-24-right-to-protest":{"newsType":true,"date":"24 July 2026","paper":"The Hindu","subject":"Polity / GS-2 (Fundamental Rights, Policing, BNSS)","summary":"An analysis of the constitutional and legal framework governing protests and police power in India, prompted by the July 20, 2026 Jantar Mantar protest (NEET-NTA related) where police used tear gas and lathicharges. The article examines Article 19(1)(b), reasonable restrictions under Article 19(3), the new BNSS's provisions on unlawful assemblies, and Supreme Court precedents on the right to protest and police accountability.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"Constitutional Framework","points":["Article 19(1)(b): guarantees citizens the right to assemble peacefully without arms","Article 19(3): permits 'reasonable restrictions' on this right in interests of public order, sovereignty and integrity of India, and other constitutionally recognised grounds","The right to protest is NOT absolute — it is subject to reasonable restrictions through laws governing public order and policing","Shaheen Bagh protest context (Amit Sahni v. Commissioner of Police, 2020): SC affirmed dissent is a constitutional right but cannot result in indefinite occupation of public spaces"]},{"heading":"BNSS (Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita) Provisions","points":["BNSS replaces CrPC (Code of Criminal Procedure); operative from 1 July 2024","Section 163 of BNSS: prohibitory orders for the New Delhi district, EXCEPT at the designated Jantar Mantar protest site with prior permission","Under BNS (Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, replacing IPC): an assembly of 5+ persons becomes 'unlawful' only if its common object falls within specific categories — using criminal force, resisting execution of law, committing an offence, compelling another person by force or threat","A gathering that begins lawfully can become unlawful if its conduct changes over the course of the event","BNSS provision: an Executive Magistrate or authorised police officer can order an unlawful assembly to disperse; if it does not comply, law permits it to be dispersed by force","BNSS requires every police officer arresting to bear an accurate, visible and clear identification of name, BUT contains NO corresponding requirement for police engaged in crowd-control or dispersal of assemblies"]},{"heading":"Standards Governing Police Action","points":["National Human Rights Commission (NHRC) Manual on Human Rights for Police Officers: effective policing and respect for human rights are complementary, not conflicting","Police Manual: democratic country needs democratic policing — police as protectors of citizens' rights, not just state enforcers","Human rights violations by police can have a 'multi-dimensional impact' — eroding public confidence and escalating civil unrest","UN Basic Principles on Use of Force and Firearms: force must be lawful, necessary, and proportionate","Code of Conduct for Police in India: use irreducible minimum force; persuasion, advice, and warning first"]},{"heading":"Supreme Court Precedents","points":["Anita Thakur v. State of J&K (2016): use of excessive force violates fundamental rights; police must be compensated, police action must remain reasonable and accountable","Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan v. Union of India (2018): authorities may regulate demonstrations to maintain public order, but CANNOT extinguish the right to protest altogether","Amit Sahni v. Commissioner of Police (2020): dissent is a constitutional right but cannot result in indefinite occupation of public spaces","In the July 20 case: Delhi High Court sought responses from Centre and Delhi Police on petitions alleging police brutality and excessive use of force","Chief Justice of India Surya Kant orally declined to take suo motu cognisance, saying 'We are not interested in videos... Don't waste our time'"]},{"heading":"Cross-links","points":["GS-2 Polity: Article 19(1)(b), Article 19(3), BNSS/BNS (successor to CrPC/IPC), fundamental rights vs reasonable restrictions","GS-2 Governance: Police reforms, accountability, Model Police Act, National Police Commission","GS-4 Ethics: Use of force on civilians, human rights, police accountability","Essay: 'Dissent is the safety valve of democracy — when it is suppressed, the pressure builds'","Cross-link: RTI article (same edition) — both deal with institutional accountability of the State"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"BNSS (Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita)","explanation":"The new Code of Criminal Procedure, operative from 1 July 2024, replacing the CrPC (Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973). Part of the three-criminal-law reform package: BNS (replacing IPC), BNSS (replacing CrPC), BSA (replacing Indian Evidence Act). Key changes: time-bound trials, video conferencing for trials, organised crime provisions, redefined terrorism offences. The BNSS's Section 163 governs prohibitory orders."},{"term":"Article 19(1)(b) — Right to Peaceful Assembly","explanation":"One of the six freedoms guaranteed under Article 19 of the Indian Constitution. Provides: 'All citizens shall have the right to assemble peaceably and without arms.' Subject to reasonable restrictions under Article 19(3): in the interests of sovereignty and integrity of India, or public order. This right is available only to CITIZENS (not to non-citizens or companies). The State can regulate (not abolish) this right through law."},{"term":"Unlawful Assembly (BNS provision)","explanation":"Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS, successor to IPC), an assembly of 5 or more persons becomes 'unlawful' only if its common object is: (1) to overawe the Central/State Government or legislature by criminal force, (2) to resist execution of any legal process, (3) to commit an offence, (4) to take/obtain possession of any property or deprive another of an easement by criminal force, (5) by criminal force to compel any person to do what they are not legally bound to do. Merely assembling for protest is NOT an unlawful assembly."},{"term":"UN Basic Principles on Use of Force and Firearms","explanation":"Adopted by the Eighth UN Congress on Prevention of Crime and Treatment of Offenders (1990). Establishes international standards for law enforcement use of force. Key principles: (1) use non-violent means first; (2) only use force when unavoidable; (3) use only proportionate force; (4) minimise damage and injury; (5) ensure medical aid for injured; (6) account and report on use of force. These are not legally binding but constitute authoritative international standards."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The right to protest is not a peripheral constitutional right — it is the operational face of Articles 19(1)(a) (free speech), 19(1)(b) (assembly), and 19(1)(c) (association). Together, they constitute the bedrock of a deliberative democracy. The SC's jurisprudence (Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan, 2018) makes clear that while the State can regulate protests to maintain public order, it cannot extinguish them.","The BNSS introduces a critical accountability gap: it requires individual police officers to bear identification when making arrests, but this requirement does NOT extend to officers engaged in crowd control or dispersal — making it difficult to identify officers who use excessive force in protest situations.","The 'proportionality' principle from the UN Basic Principles on Use of Force provides the normative framework: force used must be (a) lawful, (b) necessary (no less coercive alternative available), and (c) proportionate (not excessive relative to the threat). Tear gas and lathicharges against peaceful marchers who had not attacked anyone raises serious proportionality concerns.","Police accountability in India suffers from a structural failure: internal accountability (departmental inquiries controlled by the same police hierarchy), external accountability (NHRC and state human rights commissions with no enforcement power), and judicial accountability (slow and expensive litigation). The Model Police Act of 2006 recommended independent Police Complaints Authorities — barely any state has implemented this."],"prelims":["BNSS: operative from 1 July 2024; replaces CrPC 1973; one of three new criminal laws","BNS: replaces IPC 1860; defines unlawful assembly as 5+ persons with specific illegal common object","Article 19(1)(b): right to assemble peacefully without arms — for citizens only","Article 19(3): grounds for reasonable restriction — public order, sovereignty and integrity of India","Anita Thakur v. State of J&K (2016): SC held excessive force violates fundamental rights","Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan v. UoI (2018): can regulate but NOT extinguish right to protest","Amit Sahni v. Commissioner of Police (2020): dissent is constitutional right but not indefinite public space occupation","UN Basic Principles on Use of Force: force must be lawful, necessary, and proportionate (adopted 1990)","NHRC: National Human Rights Commission; manual emphasises human rights + effective policing are complementary"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-07-24-right-to-protest":{"title":"Right to Protest & Police Power (24 Jul 2026)","questions":[{"q":"Under which Article of the Constitution is the right to assemble peacefully guaranteed?","options":["Article 14","Article 19(1)(a)","Article 19(1)(b)","Article 21"],"correct":2,"explain":"Article 19(1)(b) guarantees all citizens the right to assemble peaceably and without arms. This right is subject to reasonable restrictions under Article 19(3) in the interests of public order and sovereignty/integrity of India."},{"q":"Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), an assembly of five or more persons becomes 'unlawful' only when:","options":["The assembly is not registered with local authorities in advance","Its common object falls within specific categories such as using criminal force or resisting execution of law","The assembly exceeds 100 persons","The assembly blocks a public road for more than one hour"],"correct":1,"explain":"Under BNS, an assembly of 5+ persons is unlawful only if its common object includes using criminal force, resisting legal processes, committing an offence, or compelling another person by force — merely assembling for protest is NOT unlawful."},{"q":"The BNSS's identification requirement for police officers has a significant gap. Which of the following correctly describes it?","options":["Police officers need not identify themselves in any situation","Police officers must identify themselves when making arrests, but this requirement does NOT extend to officers engaged in crowd-control or dispersal","Police officers must identify themselves only during night operations","The BNSS has no provision at all regarding police identification"],"correct":1,"explain":"The BNSS requires police officers to bear accurate, visible name identification when making arrests, but crucially contains NO corresponding requirement for police personnel engaged in crowd-control or dispersal of assemblies — creating a significant accountability gap."},{"q":"Assertion (A): The State can regulate but cannot extinguish the right to protest.\nReason (R): The Supreme Court in Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan v. Union of India (2018) reiterated that while authorities may regulate demonstrations to maintain public order, they cannot eliminate the right to protest altogether.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — the Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan judgment is the authoritative precedent establishing that the State's regulatory power over protests does not extend to abolishing the right itself."},{"q":"The BNSS (Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita) became operative on:","options":["15 August 2023","26 January 2024","1 July 2024","2 October 2024"],"correct":2,"explain":"The BNSS became operative on 1 July 2024, replacing the CrPC (Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973) as part of the three-criminal-law reform package."},{"q":"The UN Basic Principles on the Use of Force and Firearms require that force used by law enforcement must satisfy which three conditions?","options":["Fast, effective, and decisive","Lawful, necessary, and proportionate","Authorised, documented, and supervised","Minimal, controlled, and supervised"],"correct":1,"explain":"The UN Basic Principles (adopted 1990) require that any use of force by law enforcement be (1) lawful, (2) necessary (no less coercive alternative available), and (3) proportionate to the legitimate objective being pursued."},{"q":"In which Supreme Court case did the Court hold that dissent is a constitutional right but cannot result in the indefinite occupation of public spaces?","options":["Anita Thakur v. State of J&K (2016)","Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan v. Union of India (2018)","Amit Sahni v. Commissioner of Police (2020)","S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (1982)"],"correct":2,"explain":"In Amit Sahni v. Commissioner of Police (2020), arising from the Shaheen Bagh protests, the SC affirmed that dissent is a constitutional right but held it cannot result in the indefinite occupation of public spaces."}]}}});
